# Anything here is excluded from the build context, so `COPY . /app` cannot put
# it in the image. Two of these are not merely noise:
#
#   .env      held the developer's SQLDESK_SECRET_KEY and SQLDESK_COOKIE_SECRET
#             and was copied into every published image, where `docker pull`
#             made it readable by anyone.
#   uploads/  is where uploaded files are written at runtime. The developer's
#             own uploads shipped inside the image, and because a named volume
#             mounted over a non-empty directory is seeded from it, every fresh
#             deployment would start with those files present and no database
#             rows to match.
#
# Keep this in step with .gitignore: anything not worth committing is generally
# not worth shipping either.

# --- secrets and runtime state ---
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
/uploads/
dump.rdb

# --- version control and CI ---
.git/
.github/
/.circleci/
/.codeclimate.yml
/netlify.toml
/setup/

# --- dependencies and build output ---
node_modules/
viz-lib/node_modules/
client/.tmp/
.tmp/
client/dist
_build
.venv/
venv/
.cache
.sass-cache

# --- test and coverage artefacts ---
/.coverage
.coverage.*
/coverage.xml
coverage
.coveralls.yml
.nyc_output
client/cypress/screenshots
client/cypress/videos
/test_orders.parquet

# --- compiled bytecode, at any depth ---
#
# `*.pyc` and `__pycache__/` on their own match only the context root. Docker
# does not read a bare name as "anywhere" the way .gitignore does, so every
# nested __pycache__ was shipping -- and a .pyc wins over its .py whenever the
# recorded source mtime still matches, which it does after a file is restored
# in the same second it was changed.
#
# On 2026-10-03 that put a *mutant* into a built image: a mutation-testing run
# had briefly changed `Dashboard.is_streaming` to `self.kind != "streaming"`,
# the run compiled it, the source was restored, and the image shipped source
# that said `==` with bytecode that said `!=`. A streaming dashboard reported
# itself as ordinary, and the Add Widget picker then offered the wrong half of
# the queries. Nothing in the repository was wrong.
**/__pycache__/
**/*.pyc
**/*.pyo

# --- agent scratch space ---
#
# `.claude/` held a full git worktree -- a second copy of the project, 17 MB,
# inside every published image.
.claude/
.wolf/

# --- editor and OS noise ---
.idea
.vscode
.DS_Store
npm-debug.log
.tool-versions
.#*
*~
